{
  "schema": "d1-clip-serializer",
  "protocol_version": "v0.1",
  "spec_section": "markdown-dialect.md §5 / §6",
  "description": "Pins the Myne dialect clip serializer (serializeClipMarkdown, src/dialect/clip.ts) — the @lezer/markdown-tree → canonical-D1 emitter that re-authors best-effort markdown handed off by the browser extension (capture-extraction sub-feature, browser-extension epic; SYNC-7 authored-canonical-D1 + SYNC-11 desktop-side serializer). Each case carries a realistic Defuddle-produced markdown source (hex) and pins the byte-exact canonical-D1 output. A clip is AUTHORED content (not a C1-preserved import), so the serializer freely renormalizes bytes (canonical delimiters, collapsed whitespace, Setext→ATX, reference-link→inline, indented-code→fenced) as long as the emit re-parses to a structurally-identical tree and a second pass is a fixed point (the checkable definition of canonical D1, story 18). Cases span the fidelity floor (faithful tables / code / math), the article wedge (headings / lists / links / emphasis / callouts / tasks / footnotes / highlights), the normalize cases (Setext→ATX, ref-link→inline, alt-emphasis-delims, indented-code→fenced), and the safe-degrade cases (drop no-emit-form constructs, never broken markdown). Deterministic cross-client behavior in the property-coercion / note-template / markdown-comment class (not a codec, not cryptographic). Hand-curated by running the LIVE TS serializer over each fixture (no Rust generator — the serializer lives TS-side); the dialect Vitest runner asserts the live serializer byte-exact against these vectors. The dedicated $-tokenizer math subset (capture-extraction task 12) pins inline `$…$` legality (opener not whitespace/digit, closer not space-preceded, single-line), block `$$…$$` round-trips, the currency-stays-literal guard, and the degrade-to-inert backslash-escape path (story 11), each generated from the live serializer. A failing vector in any port (or the desktop TS impl) is a stop-the-world bug.",
